<!-- FOUNDATIONS -->
<div class="foundations-section" id="foundations">
  <div class="found-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Introduction</div>
    <div class="divider"></div>
    <h2 class="section-title">An Orientation to International Law</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">International law is the body of rules and principles that govern relations between states — and, increasingly, between states, international organisations, and individuals. Unlike domestic law, it has no central legislature or compulsory court. Its authority rests on consent, practice, and a shared sense of legal obligation. The building blocks below are where most students begin.</p>
    <div class="found-grid">
      <div class="found-card">
        <div class="num">01</div>
        <h3>What It Is</h3>
        <p>A horizontal legal order without a sovereign above states. Obligations arise mainly from what states agree to and how they actually behave, giving the field its distinctive reliance on consent and reciprocity.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="found-card">
        <div class="num">02</div>
        <h3>Sources</h3>
        <p>Article 38 of the ICJ Statute is the classic starting point: treaties, customary international law (state practice plus <em>opinio juris</em>), general principles of law, and — as subsidiary means — judicial decisions and scholarship.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="found-card">
        <div class="num">03</div>
        <h3>Subjects</h3>
        <p>States are the primary subjects, but international organisations, individuals, and other entities now hold rights and duties too — from human rights protections to individual criminal responsibility.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="found-card">
        <div class="num">04</div>
        <h3>Institutions</h3>
        <p>The United Nations and its organs, the International Court of Justice, regional courts, and specialised tribunals give the system its forums for dispute settlement, law-making, and accountability.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="found-card">
        <div class="num">05</div>
        <h3>Key Fields</h3>
        <p>The discipline branches into the law of treaties, state responsibility, the use of force, international humanitarian law, human rights, the law of the sea, and international criminal law, among others.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="found-card">
        <div class="num">06</div>
        <h3>How to Read It</h3>
        <p>Argument in international law is source-driven. Strong analysis traces a rule to its origin, tests it against state practice and case law, and applies it carefully to the facts — the method behind every good memorial.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

